'''Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart}}; "Sant Bart Metallurgical Group"), commonly abbreviated '''GMS''', is a Balboan {{w|steel}} company and the smallest of the three principal steel producers in [[Balboa]]. Unlike its larger competitors [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI) and [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL), which focus on commodity long and flat products, GMS specialises exclusively in {{w|specialty steel}} — {{w|stainless steel}}, {{w|alloy steel}}, and {{w|tool steel}} — for the domestic manufacturing, tooling, and semiconductor equipment supply chains.

GMS is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: GMS) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index. The company produces approximately 1.79 million tonnes of {{w|crude steel}} per year, accounting for 13 per cent of total Balboan steel production.

== History ==

=== Founding (1984) ===

GMS was founded in 1984 by '''Pere Masdeu i Font''', a Balboan metallurgical engineer who had worked at the [[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]] (ANB)'s Sant Bart plant. Masdeu recognised that the privatisation of the ANB in 1982 — which split the state-owned conglomerate into [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI) and [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL) — had left a gap in the Balboan market for specialty steel grades. While ACI and FBL focused on commodity products for the construction sector, no domestic producer was manufacturing {{w|stainless steel}}, {{w|alloy steel}}, or {{w|tool steel}}, which were entirely imported from Volisania and Breisland.

Masdeu secured backing from a group of Sant Bart private investors and built a new {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) plant at [[Sant Bart]], focused on specialty steel production. The company was named "Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart" after its founder and its home city.

=== Listing and growth (1992–present) ===

GMS was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1992. The listing raised capital that was used to add a second EAF and expand the specialty steel product range. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB) acquired an 8 per cent stake at the time of the listing, and the [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF) acquired a 5 per cent stake.

Through the 1990s and 2000s, GMS expanded into specialty steel grades for the growing semiconductor and automotive supply chains. The company developed {{w|stainless steel}} grades for semiconductor equipment manufacturers in [[Okaiken]] and [[Balisca]], and {{w|alloy steel}} grades for the domestic automotive industry.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 temporarily reduced demand from the manufacturing sector but GMS maintained production, supported by the recovery in semiconductor and automotive demand.

By 2026, GMS produced approximately 1.79 million tonnes of crude steel per year and employed approximately 2,500 people.

== Operations ==

GMS operates an EAF steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], in the interior highlands of Balboa. The facility comprises:

* '''2 × {{w|electric arc furnace}}s (EAF)''': Produce {{w|crude steel}} from {{w|scrap metal}}.
* '''Specialty steel {{w|rolling (metalworking)|rolling mill}}''': Produces specialty steel products in various shapes and sizes.

The Sant Bart location provides access to the [[Yalabirga]] hydropower system, which supplies the plant's electricity. As with [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL), the EAF route combined with Balboa's low hydropower costs gives GMS a significant cost advantage over international specialty steel producers. The [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] on manufactured exports further enhances the company's export competitiveness.

== Products ==

GMS is the only Balboan producer of specialty steel. Its product portfolio includes:

=== Stainless steel ===

* '''Austenitic grades''': {{w|Stainless steel#Austenitic|304 and 316 series}} — used in food processing, chemical processing, and medical equipment.
* '''Ferritic grades''': {{w|Stainless steel#Ferritic|430 series}} — used in automotive trim, appliances, and decorative applications.
* '''Martensitic grades''': {{w|Stainless steel#Martensitic|410 and 420 series}} — used in cutlery, surgical instruments, and valve components.

=== Alloy steel ===

* '''Chromium-molybdenum grades''': Used in high-temperature applications, pressure vessels, and piping.
* '''Nickel-chromium grades''': Used in aerospace, automotive, and structural applications.
* '''Vanadium grades''': Used in high-strength structural applications and tooling.

=== Tool steel ===

* '''High-speed steel''': Used in cutting tools, drills, and milling cutters.
* '''Hot-work steel''': Used in die casting, forging dies, and extrusion tooling.
* '''Cold-work steel''': Used in stamping dies, forming tools, and blanking dies.

=== Steel billets ===

GMS also produces {{w|billet|steel billets}} for sale to downstream manufacturers who further process the specialty steel into finished products.

== Raw materials ==

GMS operates entirely on the EAF route and does not require {{w|iron ore}} or {{w|coking coal}}:

* '''{{w|Scrap metal}}''': Sourced from domestic collection (approximately 60 per cent) and imports from [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]] within the [[BTPMEU]] (approximately 40 per cent). GMS uses carefully sorted and graded scrap to ensure the quality of its specialty steel products.
* '''Alloying elements''': {{w|Chromium}}, {{w|nickel}}, {{w|molybdenum}}, {{w|vanadium}}, and other {{w|alloying element}}s are imported from international suppliers.
* '''Electricity''': Supplied by the [[Yalabirga]] hydropower system.
* '''{{w|Graphite electrode}}s''': Supplied by [[Shoko Denko Balboa]] (SD Balboa), which produces synthetic graphite from [[petroleum coke]] — a byproduct of [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery.

== Ownership ==

GMS is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the company's history as a family-founded business:

* '''[[Masdeu family]]''': 20% — descendants of founder Pere Masdeu i Font; represented on the board
* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 8% — acquired at the time of the 1992 listing
* '''[[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF)''': 5% — institutional anchor investor
* '''Public float''': approximately 67% — held by institutional and retail investors

